  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Providing Alternatives to Conflict in Cattle Settlement Communities (PACSC)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The aim of this project is consistent with the Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 16- Peace, Justice and Strong Institutions. It seeks to contribute to strengthening rights holders and duty bearers&#x2019; participation to enhance sustainable co-existence between crop farmers and herders in cattle settlement communities in Sierra Leone. The project also builds the Community Action Group&#x2019;s (CAG)[1]capacity to deliver results by deriving and disseminating lessons learned from NMJD&#x2019;s experience in delivering the Promoting Sustainable Co-Existence and Healthy Living Conditions project funded by Irish Aid Programme Grant through Christian Aid &#x2013; Sierra Leone to other 4 districts in Sierra Leone namely, Falaba, Koinadugu, Tonkilili and Bombali[2]. 


[1] CAGs are community &#x2013;based established to tackle conflict and build peace in Lei Chiefdom. They comprise, women, youth, cattle rearers crop farmers and&#xA0; local chiefs especially the section and town chiefs.


[2] These districts have been targeted because they are closer to Kono-the pilot district. The districts also have very active cattle settlement communities compared to other districts in Sierra Leone.</narrative>
  </description>
